// Title: Suspicious wbadmin Backup of NTDS or Credential Registry Hives
//
// Description:
// Detects use of wbadmin.exe to back up highly sensitive credential material such as
// NTDS.dit or the SYSTEM/SECURITY registry hives. This behavior was documented in
// an Akira ransomware intrusion involving BumbleBee and AdaptixC2.
//
// MITRE ATT&CK:
// - T1003.003 OS Credential Dumping: NTDS
// - T1003.002 OS Credential Dumping: Security Account Manager
// - T1003 OS Credential Dumping
//
// False Positive Notes:
// Legitimate backup or disaster-recovery workflows on domain controllers may reference
// NTDS.dit or registry hives. Investigate the account, target path, parent process,
// execution context, and whether the activity aligns with an approved backup product.
//
// Investigation Guidance:
// - Review the initiating process tree and user/account context.
// - Inspect the backup target, especially loopback/admin shares such as \\127.0.0.1\C$.
// - Hunt for nearby execution of credential-dumping tools, remote administration,
//   archive creation, or lateral-movement activity.
// - Review subsequent access to copied NTDS/SYSTEM/SECURITY material.
// - Validate whether wbadmin was launched interactively or by an approved backup workflow.
//
// Initiating Article:
// The DFIR Report - From Bing Search to Ransomware: BumbleBee and AdaptixC2 Deliver Akira
// https://thedfirreport.com/2026/06/29/from-bing-search-to-ransomware-bumblebee-and-adaptixc2-deliver-akira-3/

let Lookback = 30d;
DeviceProcessEvents
| where Timestamp >= ago(Lookback)
| where FileName =~ "wbadmin.exe"
| where ProcessCommandLine has "start backup"
| where ProcessCommandLine has_any (
    "ntds.dit",
    @"\NTDS\",
    @"\config\SYSTEM",
    @"\config\SECURITY"
)
| project
    Timestamp,
    FileName,
    FolderPath,
    ProcessCommandLine,
    AccountName,
    InitiatingProcessFileName,
    InitiatingProcessCommandLine,
    InitiatingProcessFolderPath,
    InitiatingProcessAccountName,
    InitiatingProcessParentFileName,
    SHA256
| order by Timestamp desc
